# prime: prints 1 if the number you give it is prime, 0 if not
#
#     $ echo 97 | vonal run examples/prime.vsr
#     1
#
# Trial division, with [n, d] held on the stack throughout.
#
# Row 0 reads n and rejects n < 2 before the loop can start: DUP, push 2, LT,
# then a turn NORTH. North from row 0 wraps to row 5 on the torus, which is the
# only reason this layout fits -- a south turn there would land inside the loop
# body. Then it seeds d = 2 and drops into row 1.
#
# Row 1 is the loop, exactly as wide as the plate so running off the east edge
# wraps to its own start, entered at column 6 by a turn-east that doubles as the
# wrap point. Each pass tests d == n first (OVER OVER EQ, turn south) and only
# then n % d == 0 (OVER OVER MOD, turn south). That order matters: testing
# divisibility first would call 2 composite, since 2 % 2 == 0.
#
# Rows 2 to 5 carry the three verdicts, each to its own tail, because each must
# print a digit and then a newline and there is no room to share. Row 2 only
# passes the two loop exits further south. Prime's tail runs west along row 3 so
# it cannot collide with the column composite is travelling down; composite's
# runs east along row 4; the n < 2 rejection lands in row 5. Each tail prints
# its digit, then push 1 and push_acc 2 to make 10, then OUT_CHAR.
#
# Correct for every integer: 0, 1 and negatives by the LT guard, 2 by the
# ordering above, everything else by division. O(n) divisions, so a large prime
# is slow but finite.
%plate 11x6

+2.6  %..6  o.26  D1.6  ^1.6  o.26  v..6  o.46  o.36  o.16  o..6
%3.6  %3.6  #4.6  v2.6  o.16  #..6  >..6  %3.6  %3.6  D2.6  v1.6
o..6  o.16  o.36  v..6  o.66  o.76  o.66  o.46  o.36  o.16  v..6
o..6  o.16  o.36  v..6  ...6  +1.6  o126  o.16  +..6  o.16  <..6
o..6  o.16  o.36  >..6  o..6  +..6  o.16  o126  +1.6  ...6  o..6
o..6  o.16  o.36  o.46  >..6  o..6  +..6  o.16  o126  +1.6  ...6
